# What virtual memory holds that names this process, and what a reader of
# a memory file written by another process must do with each. See
# tests/check-vm-host-state.sh for the rule and for what each kind means.
#
# Virtual memory is otherwise position-independent by construction: a
# composite object names its storage by entity number, an index into the
# memory table, and never by address. That is what would let a memory
# file be written out and read back at a different address in a
# different process with every reference still resolving. The entries
# below are what does not carry, and they need opposite treatment:
#
#   rebuilt   a host address. It cannot be carried at all. A reader must
#             build the structure afresh and check it against what the
#             memory file names, because what indexes it was written by
#             the process that wrote the file.
#
#   handle    a number this process issued, standing for a block of state
#             held outside virtual memory. It carries, but it names
#             nothing in another process. A reader must issue the block
#             again and store the fresh handle, or leave the entity to
#             resolve to nothing -- which it does safely, since a handle
#             is checked back against the entity it was read from.
#
# Nothing is of the rebuilt kind any more. The operator table was, and it
# is no longer in virtual memory at all: a signature carries the C
# function implementing an operator and the one checking its operands,
# and those were the only host addresses the arena held. The table is the
# memory file's own storage now, so an image neither scrubs them on the
# way out nor puts them back on the way in.
#
# What still has to be held is the ORDER of the rows, and that has not
# changed: an operator object carries the opcode of its table row, so a
# reader must hold its own table to the operator names the image carries.
# Getting that wrong raises nothing -- it dispatches to the wrong
# operator.
#
# where it is                              kind      what it names
xpost_handle.h:XPOST_HANDLE_DEVICE         handle    a device's instance state
xpost_handle.h:XPOST_HANDLE_CONTENT        handle    a vector writer's accumulated content
xpost_handle.h:XPOST_HANDLE_FONT           handle    the face a font program was opened as
xpost_handle.h:XPOST_HANDLE_FILE           handle    the stream a file reaches its bytes through
